# Curator working ledgers — local research notes, intended-untracked (superio-curator
# skill). Gitignored 2026-07-25 (decruft E3) so `git add -A` can't sweep them into a
# commit. Revert these two lines if they should ever be tracked.
/RESEARCH_SOURCES.md
/docs/linux-superio-knowledge-curator.md

# Reviewer/agent probe residue. Review agents mutate the tree to test a guard and do
# not always clean up; one such file (`src/control_ofc/ui/_zzprobe_tmp.py`, holding two
# glyph-only QPushButtons) sat inside the shipped package directory reddening the
# DEC-268 accessible-name test, and polluted every source-tree grep. These patterns stop
# the next one reaching a commit — they do NOT excuse leaving it behind: sweep the tree
# before tagging.
#
# ANCHORED to the two trees where a probe does damage: src/ ships in the package,
# tests/ reddens the suite. A gitignore pattern containing no slash matches at every
# depth, so the original unanchored `_zz*` / `*_tmp.py` reached docs/, manual/,
# packaging/, assets/ and the repo root as well — everywhere they catch nothing and
# can only *silently* swallow a legitimately named file, with `git add` reporting
# success and the file never reaching a release. That failure mode is strictly worse
# than the residue these exist to catch, which the pre-tag sweep catches anyway. The
# two curator ledgers above are anchored for the same reason.
/src/**/_zz*
/src/**/*_tmp.py
/tests/**/_zz*
/tests/**/*_tmp.py

# Workflow/efficiency audit reports produced by this repo's own analysis passes.
# Same rationale as AUDIT_*.md above — internal-only, never shipped in a release tarball.
*Audit.md
